e floor is pulled from  beneath the job retention scheme we're  looking at millions  and what we have to say and what we have  been saying to  the chancellor back when he introduced  the scheme  i think i was the first to say it was  bold and courageous  and he's going to lose all of that  advantage if we don't do  something now extending the scheme like  germany have extended it for two years  at the same time though you know you're  talking there about the government needs  to step up to the plate otherwise and  you know it will be judged but at the  same time the scheme has cost  35 billion pounds this is a lot of money  obviously jobs are so important nobody  wants to see job losses  but how much longer can it continue for  as long as it takes would we sooner  those  workers costing the taxpayer 35 million  billion pound by drawing benefits on the  dole  if germany and the rest of europe can do  it why  can't our governments the truth of the  matter is that  our economy it's essential that we are  robust enough  when we come out of this virus germany  uh france they won't be looking at  wastelands in their economy but we're in  danger  of becoming wasteland uk we have to  protect jobs because without it  then the devastation that we've seen so  far will be  uh will increase uh incredibly uh  macaulay john creek said that you've  asked the prime minister for a meeting  you've written a letter they replied  not yet but i i only wrote uh earlier in  the week and so  it's been quite busy he's been busy yes  okay um  now matt hancock was also outlining um  some new uh plans  on people who need to self-isolate part  of that of course is the stick the fines  but some of it is a carrot as well this  idea that people will receive a 500  pound payment if they're on low incomes  if they  have they can't work from home is that  something you welcome of course  anything is welcome to protect workers  but of course what the chancellor needs  to do is be much more focused we need  sectoral financial packages for aviation  automotive aerospace hospitality all of  whom are in  really really trouble the government  introduced schemes to  lend money to companies through the bank  of england  it failed miserably because of the  criteria project birch was another  approach that they took to my knowledge  there's only one  company that's uh passed the criteria  the government needs to be far more  proactive and  far more imaginative than they currently  are  um now we spoke to sukhoi star morelia  in the programme  you of course didn't back him for the  leadership race but what do you make of  how he's done so far  i do back here now he's our leader uh  he's being  competent i mean i think everybody uh  putting politics to one side when they  see him  um in question time with the prime  minister the competence  of uh kia stoma really exposes  uh the prime minister so i think he's  uh he's doing okay obviously he needs to  move on in terms of the um policies  uh and the vision and you say move on  that's quite interesting isn't it  because you know he's unveiled his new  slogan  new leadership i mean it's not very  subtle is it it's a bit of a dig at  jeremy corbyn  well it's certainly got not got my  pulses running  new leadership actually it's a bit  meaningless although  uh it's a statement of fact there is a  new leader  and that new leader was elected by the  way by  uh quite overwhelmingly by our  membership including my members  on a 10-point um platform  which was a radical progressive platform  and i think i spoke to here very  recently and he was making the point  that he doesn't want to lay out  all the details of what he or labour  stands for  four years away from a general election  i can understand that but i think he  knows that fairly  soon he will have to emerge  to the public with what his vision is  and i'm  confident if he sticks to that radical  alternative that  he ran on i'm confident that that will  uh  save him well certainly in our red wall  seats it's interesting isn't it uh you  know  you're reminding us of the radical  platform that he ran  on and you know you said you know last  month uh that you know he  ran on a radical program some might say  a corbin program he has to recognize  that if the ship is he's sailing if it  lists too much to the right  it will go under are you worried that he  is moving  too far away from what you would  describe as the radical  corbyn agenda no i'm not worried at the  moment are you sure  i'm not worried at the moment but of  course we will uh  review the situation keira is in a great  great position there is real  a real wish to support him from all  wings of the labour party i mean if you  if you look at the left of the party and  how they have behaved  to care uh it's in stark contrast to the  way the right wing of the party behave  towards corbyn  so i want him to be the prime minister  next time there's a general election  and my belief is that heavy sticks to  the kind of  progressive ambitious um alternatives  that  have been developed under labour over  the last uh of five years then i think  he will be  our prime minister and if he doesn't i  think he'll be in trump  okay uh the polling may not necessarily  uh agree with you  um because it does feel as if labour is  doing pretty well under  uh kirsten much better than uh the state  of the party uh  that he uh was inherited from jeremy  corbyn so here is the latest  hugo paul september 16th and 17th labour  and the conservatives both  on 40 now two days before jeremy corbyn  stepped down as labour  as leader sorry label was on 28 that's  24 points behind  some people would say looking at the  polls he's doing all the right things to  distance himself  i think that's a well i don't know about  distancing himself from corbyn  i i thought you asked him an interesting  question about brexit  the reality of course is labor lost the  lack of last election on one issue only  and that was brexit  the conservatives formed the government  with 44 percent  extraordinary on one slogan get brexit  done  now that's why kia very cleverly is  sidestepping  the prime minister's trap to pull him  back into brexit because brexit would be  divisive  in the labour party brexit needs to be  out of the way and i think then what  you'll see if you check back with your  research is on some of the previous  polls  what policies cobin put forward on a  whole range of issues  were very popular with with the public  in particular in our red wall seats so  that's what kia has to do he's trying to  navigate his way  through a difficult situation and i want  to help him those on the left want to  help him  but he said he's listening to people  around the country which he is  and he needs to listen to the left  because without the left  uh within our movement then kia will  i'm afraid sail the ship onto the rocks  and  i don't think he'll do that i think he's  up for listening  and the unity that he looks for is  important in his pledge when he ran for  leader he said  he intended to make the moral case for  socialism  that will do me if he continues that  line okay uh len mccluskey thank you  very much always interesting to talk  thank you

